    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§60.27 & 165.70, rpld & add §§165.74 & 420.00, Pen L; amd §§170.10, 180.10 & 210.15, CP L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4147\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the penal law and the criminal procedure law, in relation to the\nstorage and destruction of goods and articles relating to the offenses\nof trademark counterfeiting and unauthorized recordings; and to repeal\ncertain provisions of the penal law relating thereto\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Penal Law\n(\"PL\") § 60.27 to entitle law enforcement agencies to restitution\nwhen they have incurred costs attended to the storage and/or\ndestruction of goods or articles seized in connection with arrests\nfor first, second or third degree trademark counterfeiting.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would add a definition of \"representative\nsample\" to PL § 165.70, the section that defines several terms\nrelated to trademark counterfeiting offenses.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would repeal and re-enact PL § 165.74, to be\nentitled \"Seizure and destruction of goods bearing counterfeit\ntrademarks.\" This comprehensive section details the procedures to be\nfollowed for the destruction of a representative sample of goods\nbearing counterfeit trademarks, including notice to the defendant;\nhearings to be held upon request of the defendant; the steps that\nmust be taken to photograph, videotape or otherwise record such goods;\nand preservation of goods after the final determination of the charges.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would repeal and re-enact PL § 420.00, \"Seizure\nand destruction of unauthorized recordings\", which would delineate\nprocedures for destroying representative samples of unauthorized\nrecordings, ones analogous to the protocols that would be established\nwith respect to goods bearing counterfeit trademarks as set forth in\nsection 3 of the bill.\n\nFinally, sections 5, 6 and 7 of the bill would make conforming changes\nto Criminal Procedure Law sections 170.10, 180.10, and 210.15\nregarding arraignment and the right to a hearing afforded to\ndefendants accused of violating misdemeanor and felony laws relating\nto counterfeit trademarks and unauthorized recordings.\n\nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\nThis City proposes to amend the Penal Law and the\nCriminal Procedure Law to allow for the destruction, after notice and\nan opportunity for a hearing, of all but a representative sample of\nseized counterfeit trademark goods or unauthorized recordings.\n\nIn lieu of destruction, where the seized goods consist of items of\napparel or footwear, such goods could be released to the trademark\nholder or a charitable organization, with the consent of the\ntrademark holder.\n\nThis bill is intended to help law enforcement officials effectively\ncombat the trade in counterfeit trademarks and unauthorized\nrecordings. The New York City Police Department has limited\n\n\nfacilities to store goods bearing a counterfeit trademark and\nunauthorized recordings after they are seized. Because of such\nlimitations, law enforcement personnel have had to delay operations\nto seize additional goods until storage space becomes available.\nSeized goods are often bulky in nature (e.g., apparel and footwear)\nand are often seized in great quantity, and prolonged retention of\nsuch illegal material requires excessive storage capacity within\nlimited space. The NYPD has had to maintain as many as 200 trailers\nholding nearly 3,000 tons of these seized goods for an extended\nperiod of time.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable\nconsideration of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after becoming a law.\n\n",
